WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:09.383
Back at the library desk, the request form has a line near the top: Item type, with four boxes to tick.

2
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:12.159
Book, Disc, Magazine, From another library.

3
00:00:12.159 --> 00:00:17.840
Because the form lists every answer, the clerk's instruction sheet has one line per box.

4
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:20.552
And the clerk knows the sheet is complete.

5
00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.724
Now picture the same form with a blank line instead of boxes.

6
00:00:24.724 --> 00:00:26.490
Someone writes: board game.

7
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:32.508
The sheet has no line for it, and nobody notices until a board game turns up at the counter.

8
00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:36.260
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

9
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:38.250
Interfaces are meant to be open.

10
00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:41.048
Anyone can add a class, and that's the point.

11
00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:43.411
So why would I ever want to stop them?

12
00:00:44.533 --> 00:00:50.029
Take the requests a member makes at the desk: borrow a book, renew a loan, return it.

13
00:00:50.029 --> 00:00:54.296
Written as an ordinary interface, Request has three classes today.

14
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:58.418
Next week someone adds a fourth, Donate, in another package.

15
00:00:58.418 --> 00:00:59.208
It compiles.

16
00:00:59.208 --> 00:01:05.793
And every if-else chain that handles requests skips it without a word, and falls into its last else.

17
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:09.770
That last else is the code's way of saying: this can't happen.

18
00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:10.884
Famous last words.

19
00:01:10.884 --> 00:01:12.865
The trouble isn't the new class.

20
00:01:12.865 --> 00:01:15.773
It's that nothing told us the list had changed.

21
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:19.113
So let's print the boxes on the form.

22
00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:27.049
A sealed type is a class or interface that names its permitted subtypes, the only classes allowed to extend it directly.

23
00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:27.908
Let's map it.

24
00:01:27.908 --> 00:01:30.355
The interface is the line, Item type.

25
00:01:30.355 --> 00:01:33.000
Each permitted subtype is a printed box.

26
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.439
The list is in ink, fixed when the form was printed.

27
00:01:36.439 --> 00:01:40.671
And the clerk's instruction sheet is a switch, one line per box.

28
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.364
In Java, you add sealed and a permits list.

29
00:01:44.364 --> 00:01:49.571
In Kotlin, you write sealed interface, and the subtypes live in the same package.

30
00:01:49.733 --> 00:01:53.359
Now add Donate again, without putting it on the list.

31
00:01:53.359 --> 00:01:54.248
What happens?

32
00:01:54.248 --> 00:01:55.343
Pause and guess.

33
00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:07.751
javac, the Java compiler, stops: class is not allowed to extend sealed class: Request, as it is not listed in its permits clause.

34
00:02:08.667 --> 00:02:10.272
And it's not only javac.

35
00:02:10.272 --> 00:02:18.633
The list is written into the class file, and the JVM, the program that runs Java code, checks it again when it loads a class.

36
00:02:18.633 --> 00:02:21.977
Nobody gets to scribble a fifth box in the margin.

37
00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:28.986
Each permitted subtype must also say how its own branch ends, with one of three words.

38
00:02:28.986 --> 00:02:32.189
Final ends it, and records are final already.

39
00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:38.664
Sealed closes it again one level down, like Disc, with exactly two kinds: DVD and audio CD.

40
00:02:38.664 --> 00:02:41.155
And non-sealed opens it on purpose.

41
00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:45.880
Non-sealed is the box From another library, with a blank line after it.

42
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:53.180
The clerk can't list every partner's items, but one line covers them all: send it back to the library named there.

43
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.238
So one case in a switch covers every partner class, even one written next year.

44
00:02:58.238 --> 00:03:00.351
Kotlin has no non-sealed keyword.

45
00:03:00.351 --> 00:03:03.873
A plain interface under the sealed one plays that part.

46
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:05.814
Now the reward.

47
00:03:05.814 --> 00:03:11.833
Here's where one copy of a book is right now: on the shelf, on loan, on hold, or missing.

48
00:03:11.833 --> 00:03:13.930
Three functions switch over it.

49
00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:16.432
Two list every case, with no default.

50
00:03:16.432 --> 00:03:19.745
The third, reminderDays, ends with default, zero.

51
00:03:19.933 --> 00:03:22.870
Run it, and each copy gets its line.

52
00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.318
On loan, a reminder in 9 days.

53
00:03:25.318 --> 00:03:26.460
On hold, in 3.

54
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:32.125
Now the library starts sending damaged books away for repair, and someone adds InRepair.

55
00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:35.578
Which of the three switches will the compiler point at?

56
00:03:35.578 --> 00:03:36.583
Pause and guess.

57
00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:39.880
Two.

58
00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:47.945
The label and canLendToday switches stop compiling: the switch expression does not cover all possible input values.

59
00:03:47.945 --> 00:03:51.311
That's a to-do list, handed to you by the build.

60
00:03:51.467 --> 00:03:52.607
And reminderDays?

61
00:03:52.607 --> 00:03:54.486
It compiles without a sound.

62
00:03:54.486 --> 00:04:01.599
A copy in repair falls into the default and gets zero reminder days, though the repair has a due date too.

63
00:04:01.733 --> 00:04:07.903
In that switch, default really means: and anything else, which I promise never to look at.

64
00:04:07.903 --> 00:04:13.181
The OpenJDK team's own guide has a section called: By default, avoid default.

65
00:04:13.181 --> 00:04:16.677
A default on a list that grows hides the next item.

66
00:04:16.677 --> 00:04:17.568
Leave it out.

67
00:04:18.467 --> 00:04:19.574
A few guardrails.

68
00:04:19.574 --> 00:04:24.326
Seal only a list you own and compile together, so keep it in one package.

69
00:04:24.326 --> 00:04:28.103
A plug-in point that other people extend should stay open.

70
00:04:28.103 --> 00:04:33.442
And a program compiled against the old list can still meet a new item at run time.

71
00:04:33.442 --> 00:04:35.460
That's the next lesson's story.

72
00:04:36.333 --> 00:04:38.558
So, why stop people adding classes?

73
00:04:38.558 --> 00:04:41.927
Because a printed list is one the compiler can check.

74
00:04:41.927 --> 00:04:46.059
Seal the list, end each branch on purpose, and leave default out.

75
00:04:46.059 --> 00:04:51.144
Then the next new box arrives as a to-do list, not as a surprise at the counter.

76
00:04:51.333 --> 00:04:52.182
Quick check.

77
00:04:52.182 --> 00:04:58.550
A sealed class Extension, not abstract, has two final subclasses, FixedDays and SameAgain.

78
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:01.876
A switch has one case for each, and no default.

79
00:05:01.876 --> 00:05:03.008
Does it compile?

80
00:05:06.267 --> 00:05:06.483
No.

81
00:05:06.483 --> 00:05:13.708
Extension isn't abstract, so a plain Extension is a possible value too, and neither case matches it.

82
00:05:13.708 --> 00:05:16.164
Make it abstract, or an interface.
